      "id": "2c19c41f-edec-4870-b26f-9d4c7cdafd4e",
      "text": "An **entity** declares the interface; an **architecture** describes the hardware implementing it. Concurrent assignments describe connections that exist together, rather than a list of software operations executed once from top to bottom.",
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "citations": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "b1138b61-062f-4390-b0f6-ca70268c4f00",
          "source_id": "a03a4d55-8676-487c-96f7-77757c75cb49",
          "version": 1
        },
        {
          "chunk_id": "f4e3486d-0c5e-4631-b7c6-7223c71d4528",
          "source_id": "a03a4d55-8676-487c-96f7-77757c75cb49",
          "version": 1
        }
      ],
      "id": "a7c19937-ebb3-4ad7-872c-0036128118f9",
      "language": "vhdl",
      "text": "library ieee;\nuse ieee.std_logic_1164.all;\n\nentity logic_example is\n  port (a, b, c : in std_logic;\n        f       : out std_logic);\nend entity;\n\narchitecture rtl of logic_example is\nbegin\n  f <= b or (a and not c);\nend architecture;",
      "type": "code"
    },
    {
      "citations": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "b1138b61-062f-4390-b0f6-ca70268c4f00",
          "source_id": "a03a4d55-8676-487c-96f7-77757c75cb49",
          "version": 1
        }
      ],
      "id": "72f16c83-e8b7-4ee3-a373-e978dabc5ba5",
      "text": "`std_logic` includes 0 and 1 plus simulation states such as `U` (uninitialized), `X` (unknown), and `Z` (high impedance). A Boolean truth-table proof checks the binary cases; it does not establish behavior for every simulation state.",
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "citations": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "f869cb46-131d-4bf6-b27b-e1944ecf241b",
          "source_id": "a03a4d55-8676-487c-96f7-77757c75cb49",
          "version": 1
        }
      ],
      "id": "a82ff7d3-4992-456d-b76f-84bd09613ac4",
      "text": "The design flow is specification \u2192 schematic/HDL \u2192 synthesis and functional verification \u2192 physical implementation and timing verification. A correct truth table does not guarantee that a physical implementation meets timing.",
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "answer": "No. Both connections exist concurrently. Ordering inside a process is a separate question.",
      "citations": [],
      "difficulty": "intermediate",
      "id": "3f0f4bcf-2101-487b-ba3e-281c27112de1",
      "origin": "original",
      "prompt": "Does exchanging the order of `f <= a and b;` and `g <= not a;` change these two outputs?",
      "solution": "",
      "steps": [
        {
          "explanation": "Each concurrent assignment defines its own logic; neither reads the other output.",
          "latex": ""
        }
      ],
      "title": "Try it: two outputs are concurrent",
      "type": "practice_problem"
    },
    {
      "citations": [],
      "id": "02a6467c-7d06-4dec-b29f-7b9dcc9a4b27",
      "text": "The HDL examples in these notes are teaching descriptions. Their binary behavior is checked with the corresponding equations; no HDL simulator or synthesis run is claimed.",
