      "id": "79a1ea54-06ad-4ca1-bbb1-f9ae3b326783",
      "text": "**Propagation delay** is the time between an input transition and the resulting output transition. **Fan-in** counts a gate\u2019s inputs; **fan-out** describes the load it drives. More loading can increase delay, so fewer gates need not mean the fastest circuit.",
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "citations": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "b1fe982d-865e-4d16-8117-3aef88f19ee0",
          "source_id": "43433cc2-ac9b-4f48-9f8f-e352ed8b54e2",
          "version": 1
        }
      ],
      "id": "12f048e1-41cc-4847-9a8a-3e072419b489",
      "text": "A buffer reproduces its input and can provide drive. A **tri-state buffer** drives 0 or 1 when enabled and disconnects its output when disabled; that disconnected state is **Z**, not logic 0. A shared bus needs controls that prevent conflicting drivers.",
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "answer": "No; the bus has contention. With both drivers disabled, the bus is undriven unless another circuit establishes its level.",
      "citations": [],
      "difficulty": "intermediate",
      "id": "57a2e798-bc2b-4a95-940b-e6b3d1f5379b",
      "origin": "original",
      "prompt": "Driver A drives 1 and driver B drives 0 onto the same bus. Is the bus value an OR of their outputs?",
      "solution": "",
      "steps": [
        {
          "explanation": "Both outputs actively try to impose different voltages. Boolean OR does not model this connection.",
          "latex": ""
        },
        {
          "explanation": "Disable one driver to Z before allowing the other to drive.",
          "latex": ""
        }
      ],
      "title": "Try it: tri-state bus control",
      "type": "practice_problem"
    },
