      ],
      "id": "0c48cb28-5057-4b1b-a121-8d6560abcc79",
      "text": "Group bits because these bases are powers of two.\n\n- Base 4: 2 bits per digit.\n- Base 8: 3 bits per digit.\n- Base 16: 4 bits per digit; A\u2013F mean decimal 10\u201315.\n\nStart each grouping at the radix point and work outward. Pad the far left of an integer or far right of a fraction with zeroes.",
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "answer": "$(\\mathrm{B6})_{16}=(266)_8=182$. Slide 43 prints 226 in its octal result; the grouping gives 266.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "306c2d70-d7a0-4134-bb40-4d841072b4a5",
          "source_id": "ff6535b5-436e-42d5-8e1e-da0e01122696",
          "version": 1
        }
      ],
      "id": "b5c23703-a945-419a-9277-97a59ab95df2",
      "prompt": "Convert $(10110110)_2$ to hex and octal.",
      "steps": [
        {
          "explanation": "Group by four for hex.",
          "latex": "1011\\;0110\\Rightarrow(\\mathrm{B6})_{16}"
        },
        {
          "explanation": "Group by three for octal, padding only at the far left.",
          "latex": "010\\;110\\;110\\Rightarrow(266)_8"
        },
        {
          "explanation": "Check the value independently.",
          "latex": "2(8^2)+6(8)+6=182"
        }
      ],
      "title": "One pattern, two compact forms",
      "type": "worked_example"
    },
    {
      "answer": "$(157)_{16}$; both forms equal 343.",
      "citations": [
